  "icbcText": "Initial conditions define initial sea level, currents, salinity, temperature, density stratification, turbulence and tracer concentration. Boundary equations include tidal-elevation boundaries, prescribed current/velocity boundaries, open-radiation boundaries, Flather-type boundaries, discharge-flow boundaries, brine salinity/temperature source conditions, seabed no-normal-flow and friction conditions, free-surface kinematic/dynamic conditions, atmospheric heat-flux conditions, wind-stress conditions and zero-gradient/outflow conditions.",
  "calibration": "Calibrate ambient mixing coefficients, horizontal and vertical diffusivities, bottom roughness, turbulence parameters, entrainment coefficients, diffuser discharge coefficients and far-field dispersion parameters against site-specific current-meter, CTD, ADCP, tide-gauge, drifter, dye-tracer and salinity-plume observations.",
  "verification": "Verify conservation of water, salt, momentum and buoyancy; test quiescent receiving-water cases, uniform-current cases, stratified-water benchmarks, single-port and multiport diffuser benchmarks, grid and time-step convergence, domain-size independence and near-field/far-field coupling. Relevant names include Reynolds-Averaged Navier-Stokes Equations, Advection-Diffusion Equation, Boussinesq Approximation, Buoyant-Jet Integral Equations, Densimetric Froude Number Relations, Continuity Equation and Salt Conservation Equation.",
  "validation": "Compare predicted dilution, centreline salinity, plume width, plume thickness, trajectory, terminal rise/fall height, seabed impingement, mixing-zone dimensions and excess-salinity footprint with independent field surveys. Validate under different tides, current directions, stratification states and seasonal conditions because agreement during one survey does not establish general validity.",
  "inputs": "Marine brine-discharge modelling requires information describing both the discharge itself and the receiving environment. Brine inputs include discharge flow rate, salinity, temperature, density, total dissolved solids and concentrations of environmentally relevant chemicals or residual treatment substances. The outfall description requires geographical position, water depth, pipe diameter, diffuser length, number and spacing of discharge ports, port diameter, port orientation, discharge angle and exit velocity. Marine inputs include ambient seawater temperature, salinity and density throughout the water column, current velocity and direction, tides, waves, wind conditions, turbulence and vertical stratification. Detailed simulations additionally require seabed bathymetry, coastline geometry, seabed roughness, computational-domain boundaries and suitable spatial resolution. Long-duration studies can require seasonal variations in seawater temperature, salinity, currents, tides and meteorological conditions. These data enable assessment of dilution, plume movement, seabed exposure, recirculation and the spatial extent of elevated salinity.",
  "outputs": "The required output should describe the three-dimensional and time-dependent fate of the discharged brine from the diffuser through the near field and into the surrounding marine environment. It should report brine-plume trajectory, plume width, height, thickness, length and footprint, together with dilution as a function of distance from the discharge. Spatial maps should show salinity, excess salinity above ambient seawater, temperature, density and, where modelled, concentrations of residual treatment chemicals or other constituents. The results should identify maximum seabed salinity, maximum water-column salinity, the distance required to reach specified dilution or environmental thresholds and the area of seabed or water column exposed above those thresholds. Outputs should show how the plume changes with tides, currents, waves, seasonal temperature and salinity stratification and different plant discharge rates. They should also identify dense brine accumulation, recirculation, trapping in depressions and possible return of discharged brine toward the desalination intake. Environmental reporting should include worst-case conditions, duration and frequency of threshold exceedances, affected area and predicted exposure at environmentally sensitive receptors.",
