  "icbcText": "Initial conditions specify stage/effect temperatures, pressures, salinities, liquid inventories, vapour inventories and wall temperatures. Boundary equations include feed-flow, feed-temperature and feed-salinity conditions, heating-steam pressure/temperature conditions, condenser cooling-water conditions, terminal vacuum/pressure conditions, heat-flux conditions, convective heat-transfer conditions, vapour–liquid interfacial equilibrium conditions and product/brine outlet conditions.",
  "calibration": "Calibrate overall heat-transfer coefficients, boiling-point elevation corrections, non-equilibrium allowances, demister efficiency, heat losses, fouling factors, condenser performance, compressor efficiency, ejector performance and stage/effect pressure losses against pilot or plant data over different loads and seawater temperatures.",
  "verification": "Close mass, salt and energy balances around every effect/stage and the complete system; verify steam and condensate accounting, thermodynamic property consistency, limiting cases, heat-exchanger calculations and convergence with increasing stage/effect resolution. Relevant names include First-Law Energy Balance, Mass Balance, Salt Balance, Antoine Equation, Clausius-Clapeyron Equation, Boiling-Point Elevation Relations, Log-Mean Temperature Difference Method, Effectiveness-NTU Method and Vapour-Liquid Equilibrium.",
  "validation": "Validate distillate production, brine concentration, stage temperatures, stage pressures, steam consumption, condenser duty, gain-output ratio, performance ratio, specific thermal energy and electrical consumption against independent pilot/full-scale data, including seasonal seawater-temperature and part-load conditions.",
  "inputs": "Thermal-desalination modelling requires feed-water flow, salinity, chemical composition, temperature, pressure, density, heat capacity, thermal conductivity, viscosity and boiling characteristics. Heating-system inputs include steam flow, steam temperature and pressure, heating-medium conditions, cooling-water temperature and flow, condenser pressure, vacuum conditions and estimated heat losses. The configuration requires the number of effects or stages, their operating temperatures and pressures, heat-transfer areas, overall heat-transfer coefficients, feed distribution and pressure losses. Flashing systems additionally require individual stage conditions and brine-recirculation parameters. Vapour-compression systems require compressor pressure ratio, compressor efficiency, motor efficiency and vapour conditions. Other important inputs can include fouling factors, non-condensable gas concentrations, demister performance, condenser characteristics and heat-exchanger effectiveness.",
  "outputs": "The required output should provide freshwater production and detailed thermal performance for every stage or effect. It should report feed, brine, vapour and distillate flow rates; temperature and pressure at each stage; evaporation and condensation rates; stage-wise salinity; final concentrate salinity; and overall recovery. Heat-transfer outputs should include heating-steam consumption, heat duties, heat-transfer coefficients, temperature differences, required heat-transfer areas and condenser loads. Performance indicators should include gained-output ratio, performance ratio, specific thermal-energy consumption, specific electrical-energy consumption and total specific energy consumption. For mechanically or thermally driven vapour compression, compressor or ejector performance, compression ratio, motive-steam consumption and electrical power should also be reported. The output should identify boiling-point elevation, heat losses, scale-prone operating regions, condenser performance and changes in efficiency caused by fouling. Plant-level results should state product-water quality, total water production, brine production, cooling-water requirement, steam requirement and expected performance over the specified operating range.",
