  "validation": "Validate diluate and concentrate ion concentrations, salt removal, current density, stack voltage, current efficiency, water transport, membrane potential, limiting current density and specific energy consumption against independent stack experiments over different salinities, flow rates, currents and temperatures.",
  "inputs": "Electrochemical desalination requires the feed composition to be specified ion by ion because individual charged species determine electrical transport. Inputs include the concentration and charge of significant cations and anions, ionic diffusivities, ionic mobilities, solution conductivity, pH, temperature, density and feed flow. Membrane information includes membrane thickness, ion-exchange capacity, fixed-charge characteristics, water content, electrical resistance, conductivity, porosity, ion diffusivity, selectivity, co-ion exclusion and water permeability. Stack geometry requires membrane area, channel height, spacer characteristics, number of cell pairs and the arrangement of dilute and concentrate channels. Electrical inputs include applied voltage, current, electrode properties, electrode-rinse conditions and electrical contact resistance. Flow velocity, pressure drop and residence time are also needed. More advanced studies can require electro-osmotic water transport, osmotic transport, fouling parameters and limiting-current information.",
  "outputs": "The required output should report ion-by-ion transport through the membrane system and how this changes along the flow channels and across successive cell pairs. It should provide diluate and concentrate flow rates and compositions, salt removal, water recovery, desalination efficiency and final product-water salinity. Electrical outputs should include applied voltage, current, current density, electrical resistance, membrane potential, voltage drop across individual compartments and total electrical power consumption. Ion-transport results should give fluxes of individual cations and anions, membrane selectivity, current efficiency, charge efficiency and unwanted co-ion transport. The results should identify concentration polarization, limiting-current conditions, water splitting, local pH changes and locations where precipitation or membrane scaling may occur. They should also report specific energy consumption, membrane area required, number of cell pairs or stacks, pressure losses, pumping demand and performance as functions of feed salinity, flow rate and electrical operating conditions.",
