  "calibration": "For forward osmosis, calibrate water permeability, solute permeability, structural parameter, external and internal concentration-polarization parameters and reverse-solute flux. For membrane distillation, calibrate membrane porosity, tortuosity, pore size, thermal conductivity, mass-transfer coefficient, heat-transfer coefficient and temperature-polarization coefficient. For other emerging processes, calibrate technology-specific transport, adsorption, electrochemical and kinetic parameters against controlled experiments.",
  "verification": "Test water, solute and energy conservation, zero-driving-force limits, membrane symmetry where applicable, analytical transport limits and spatial/time-step independence. Relevant names include Solution-Diffusion Model, Internal Concentration-Polarization Model, External Concentration-Polarization Model, Van't Hoff Equation, Dusty-Gas Model, Knudsen Diffusion Model, Molecular Diffusion Model, Antoine Equation, Kelvin Equation and Heat-Conduction Equation.",
  "validation": "Validate water flux, reverse-solute flux, draw-solution dilution, rejection, membrane-surface temperatures, thermal efficiency, temperature polarization, vapour flux, wetting onset and specific energy consumption using independent experiments spanning concentration, temperature, flow velocity and membrane type.",
  "inputs": "Forward-osmosis modelling requires the compositions, concentrations, temperatures and flow rates of both the feed and draw solutions. Membrane information includes water permeability, solute permeability, membrane thickness, structural characteristics, active-layer properties, support-layer porosity and tortuosity. Mass-transfer coefficients and information describing internal and external concentration polarization are also required. Membrane-distillation modelling instead places greater emphasis on feed and coolant temperatures and flows, salinity, membrane thickness, pore size and pore-size distribution, porosity, tortuosity, thermal conductivity, contact angle and resistance to liquid penetration. The module geometry, effective membrane area and thermal boundary conditions must also be specified. Other emerging processes may require electrode properties, electrical conditions, specialized membrane properties or additional chemical and transport information. Hybrid systems additionally require the outlet conditions of one process to be properly defined as the inlet conditions of the next.",
  "outputs": "The required outputs should be tailored to the process but should provide water production, solute transport, membrane behaviour, energy demand and the evolution of both feed and receiving streams. For forward osmosis, outputs should include water flux, reverse solute flux, feed concentration, draw-solution concentration, dilution of the draw solution, concentration polarization, membrane selectivity, water recovery and subsequent draw-solution regeneration requirements. For membrane distillation, the results should include vapour flux, distillate production, salt rejection, feed and permeate temperatures, membrane-surface temperatures, temperature polarization, heat flux, conductive heat losses, thermal efficiency and the risk of membrane wetting. Other emerging processes should report their appropriate ion, solvent, thermal or electrical transport quantities, together with water recovery and product quality. Hybrid systems should additionally show how material and energy pass between the constituent processes, the performance contribution of each process, total freshwater production, overall recovery, brine reduction and combined specific energy consumption.",
