  "icbcText": "Initial conditions specify brine composition, supersaturation, temperature, pressure, initial crystal population, seed size distribution and initial solid fraction. Boundary equations include brine-feed concentration/flow conditions, heat-flux or temperature conditions, evaporation/vapour-flux conditions, crystallizer wall no-flux conditions, crystal-wall deposition conditions, outlet population-flux conditions and solid–liquid equilibrium/interface conditions.",
  "calibration": "Calibrate solubility behaviour, supersaturation thresholds, metastable-zone width, nucleation kinetics, crystal-growth kinetics, agglomeration, breakage, crystal-size-dependent growth, evaporation coefficients, heat-transfer coefficients and fouling/scaling parameters using controlled crystallization, evaporation and concentrated-brine experiments.",
  "verification": "Close water, elemental, ionic, solid and energy balances; verify phase equilibrium, precipitation sequence, crystal population conservation, idealized batch-crystallizer benchmarks and grid/time-step independence for population-balance calculations. Relevant names include Population Balance Equation, Classical Nucleation Theory, Crystal-Growth Rate Laws, Gibbs Free-Energy Minimization, Solubility-Product Relations, Pitzer Model, Species Mass Balance and Energy Balance.",
  "validation": "Validate evaporation rate, concentration factor, precipitation onset, mineral identity, salt yield, mother-liquor composition, crystal-size distribution, water recovery, heat consumption and final solids production against independent pilot or demonstration-scale measurements, using XRD and chemical analysis to verify predicted solid phases.",
  "inputs": "Highly concentrated brine modelling requires a rigorous initial chemical analysis including total flow, temperature, pressure, pH, alkalinity, water content and concentrations of sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium, barium, strontium, chloride, sulphate, carbonate species, silica, boron and other relevant dissolved constituents. Dissolved organics and treatment chemicals should also be included when significant. Thermodynamic inputs must identify possible dissolved and solid phases and provide suitable equilibrium and solubility information. Evaporation processes require heating conditions, operating pressure, heat-transfer characteristics, evaporation rate and heat-loss information. Crystallization additionally requires supersaturation conditions, nucleation behaviour, crystal-growth characteristics, residence time, seed-crystal properties and particle-size information. Selective mineral-recovery studies require reagent dosages, target pH conditions and desired precipitation sequence. Complete zero-liquid-discharge studies also require recycle ratios, purge conditions, solids-separation characteristics, dryer conditions and target overall water recovery.",
  "outputs": "The required output should provide a complete account of water recovery and solid formation as brine is progressively concentrated. It should report feed-brine flow and composition, concentrate flow, evaporated or recovered water, final residual-liquid volume and total water recovery. Chemical outputs should show dissolved-ion concentrations throughout concentration, saturation conditions for relevant minerals, the point at which individual salts begin to precipitate and the order in which solids form. The model should quantify the mass, composition and purity of every precipitated solid, crystal production rate, residual dissolved concentration and, where modelled, crystal-size distribution and crystal-growth characteristics. Evaporator and crystallizer outputs should include operating temperatures and pressures, heat duties, steam requirements, electrical consumption and heat-transfer performance. The final results should distinguish recovered freshwater, potentially saleable salts or minerals, mixed solids requiring disposal and any residual liquid waste. For a true zero-liquid-discharge design, the output should demonstrate the final water and solids balance and confirm whether any liquid discharge remains.",
