  "verification": "Verify normalization calculations, mass conservation, resistance-in-series implementation, limiting behaviour at zero foulant concentration, synthetic degradation cases, time-step independence and exact recovery of known injected faults. Relevant names include Darcy's Law, Resistance-in-Series Model, Hermia Blocking Laws, Cake-Filtration Model, Standard Blocking Model, Intermediate Blocking Model, Complete Blocking Model, Langmuir Adsorption Model and Monod Kinetics.",
  "validation": "Validate normalized permeate flow, normalized salt passage, transmembrane pressure, differential pressure, fouling rate, cleaning requirement, cleaning recovery and remaining membrane performance against independent operating periods and membrane-autopsy observations. For predictive monitoring, validation must be chronological/out-of-sample to avoid information leakage.",
  "inputs": "Fouling and membrane-performance monitoring primarily requires continuous or regularly sampled operating data. Important inputs include feed, concentrate and permeate flow rates; feed, interstage, concentrate and permeate pressures; pressure drop across each membrane stage; feed and permeate conductivity; feed temperature; pH; recovery; and actual product-water production. Water-quality measurements should include turbidity, silt density index, total and dissolved organic carbon, microbial indicators, silica, hardness, alkalinity, iron, manganese and other potential foulants. Operational records should include antiscalant, coagulant, acid, chlorine and dechlorination dosages, membrane age, operating hours, shutdowns and membrane replacements. Cleaning records should identify cleaning dates, cleaning chemicals, concentrations, temperatures, pH, duration and membrane performance before and after cleaning. More advanced predictive models can additionally require membrane resistance, deposition rates, cake properties, biofilm-growth characteristics and empirical fouling parameters.",
  "outputs": "The required output should provide current and normalized membrane-performance indicators and their evolution with operating time. It should report normalized permeate flow, normalized salt passage or rejection, normalized differential pressure, membrane recovery, permeability, flux, feed pressure, concentrate pressure, permeate pressure and product-water conductivity or salinity. The system should distinguish changes caused by temperature, feed salinity and normal operating adjustments from genuine deterioration of membrane performance. Trend outputs should identify the rate of permeability loss, increase in salt passage, pressure-drop growth and changes in individual stages or pressure vessels. Where sufficient measurements are available, the results should estimate the probability or severity of particulate fouling, organic fouling, biological fouling and mineral scaling. Monitoring outputs should identify abnormal elements or stages, provide early-warning indicators, detect sudden versus gradual degradation and estimate when cleaning thresholds will be reached. Following cleaning, the output should quantify performance recovery and determine whether deterioration is reversible or indicative of permanent membrane damage.",
