  "icbcText": "Initial conditions include initial decision variables, state vectors, model parameters, neural-network parameters, prior distributions, covariance matrices and controller states. Boundary/constraint equations include physical conservation constraints, parameter bounds, operating envelopes, water-quality constraints, pressure constraints, recovery constraints, energy constraints, scaling constraints, terminal-state constraints, PDE boundary constraints and feasibility constraints.",
  "calibration": "For physics models, estimate uncertain parameters using independent experimental/plant data and identifiability analysis. For machine learning, tune hyperparameters using training and validation partitions while preserving a genuinely untouched test dataset; use nested cross-validation where model selection is extensive, time-series splits for temporal data and grouped/site-wise splits when multiple observations come from the same plant. Probability models should additionally undergo probability calibration.",
  "verification": "Verify code against manufactured solutions, analytical solutions, conservation laws and benchmark problems; perform unit, integration, regression and property-based tests; test optimization algorithms on known benchmark functions; repeat stochastic optimizations with multiple seeds; verify gradients where gradient-based optimization is used; and establish solver convergence and constraint satisfaction. Relevant names include Method of Manufactured Solutions, Richardson Extrapolation, Karush-Kuhn-Tucker Conditions, Bayesian Optimization, Gaussian Process Regression, Backpropagation, Gradient Descent, Monte Carlo Method and Bayesian Inference.",
  "validation": "Use unseen data and report task-appropriate metrics such as RMSE, MAE, coefficient of determination, classification precision/recall where applicable, calibration curves and prediction intervals. Perform external validation on different operating periods, plants or geographical sites where possible; test robustness to noise, missing sensors and distribution shift; conduct uncertainty quantification, sensitivity analysis, ablation studies and comparison against simple baselines. A model should not be called independently validated when the same observations were used to tune it.",
  "inputs": "Optimization and artificial-intelligence applications require input datasets tailored to the specific quantity being predicted or optimized. Design variables can include operating pressure, flow, recovery, membrane configuration, number of stages, recycle fraction, chemical dosage, pump speed, energy-recovery settings and cleaning frequency. Fixed inputs include feed-water chemistry, temperature, equipment characteristics and physical constraints. Operating constraints must define maximum pressure, acceptable water quality, permissible scaling, minimum production, maximum recovery, equipment capacities and environmental limits. The optimization objective must also be explicitly specified, such as minimizing energy consumption, water-production cost, brine generation or emissions, or maximizing recovery and membrane lifetime. Data-driven models require historical timestamped measurements of chemistry, pressure, flow, conductivity, temperature, dosing, energy consumption, membrane condition, maintenance and observed plant outputs. Numerical models additionally require governing physical relationships, initial and boundary conditions, material properties, spatial resolution, time-step requirements, convergence criteria and uncertainty ranges. Uncertainty and probabilistic optimization also require parameter distributions, ranges and correlations.",
  "outputs": "The required output should provide not only a single predicted or optimum operating condition but also the evidence needed to understand its robustness and limitations. Optimization outputs should report the recommended operating pressure, recovery, flow, membrane configuration, chemical dosage, energy-recovery settings and other controllable variables, together with the resulting water production, water quality, energy use, scaling risk and operating cost. Multi-objective studies should provide the trade-off between competing objectives such as minimum energy, minimum cost, maximum recovery, minimum brine production and minimum environmental impact, rather than hiding these compromises behind one solution. Predictive models should report predicted permeate flow, salt rejection, fouling, scaling, energy consumption, maintenance requirements or other target variables together with prediction errors, confidence or uncertainty estimates and performance on independent validation data. Sensitivity analysis should rank the variables controlling the results, while uncertainty analysis should provide distributions, confidence intervals and probabilities of violating operational limits. Dynamic models should provide future trajectories, anomaly detection, failure warnings and remaining time before important operating limits are reached.",
