  "icbcText": "Initial conditions specify initial pressure, flow, shaft speed, valve position, reservoir level, accumulator state and rotating-machine state. Boundary equations include prescribed upstream/downstream pressure, prescribed flow, reservoir-head boundary, pump characteristic-curve boundary, turbine characteristic boundary, pressure-exchanger interface conditions, valve-loss boundary, no-flow boundary and transient characteristic/water-hammer boundary conditions.",
  "calibration": "Calibrate pump efficiency curves, motor efficiency, variable-speed-drive efficiency, pressure-exchanger efficiency, leakage/slip, turbine efficiency, bearing/mechanical losses, hydraulic resistance and degradation coefficients against certified manufacturer curves, factory acceptance tests and calibrated plant instrumentation.",
  "verification": "Verify continuity, pressure and mechanical-energy balances; check pump affinity-law behaviour, zero-flow/shutoff and design-point limits where applicable, unit consistency and independent hand calculations at selected operating points. Relevant names include Bernoulli Equation, Mechanical Energy Equation, Darcy-Weisbach Equation, Pump Affinity Laws, Euler Turbomachinery Equation, Hydraulic Power Equation and Energy Conservation Equation.",
  "validation": "Compare predicted flow, suction/discharge pressures, differential pressure, shaft/electrical power, pump efficiency, energy-recovery efficiency and net specific energy consumption against independent measurements across the operating envelope, including partial load and variable-speed operation.",
  "inputs": "Pump and energy-recovery calculations require fluid flow rate, suction pressure, discharge pressure, required pressure increase, density, viscosity and temperature. Pump information includes characteristic performance curves, rotational speed, impeller dimensions, hydraulic efficiency, motor efficiency, variable-speed-drive efficiency, minimum operating flow and cavitation-related data. The hydraulic network requires pipe diameters and lengths, internal roughness, bends, fittings, valves, elevation changes and associated pressure losses. Energy-recovery equipment additionally requires high-pressure brine flow and pressure, low-pressure feed conditions, equipment efficiency, leakage, mixing, internal pressure losses and booster-pump requirements. Variable-operation studies also require time-dependent plant production, membrane pressure requirements, pump speeds and equipment operating points. These inputs allow the complete hydraulic and electrical energy requirement of the desalination process to be established.",
  "outputs": "The required output should provide a complete hydraulic and energy account from feed intake through high-pressure pumping and energy recovery. It should report flow rate, suction and discharge pressures, differential pressure, pump head, shaft power, electrical input power, motor efficiency, pump efficiency and operating point relative to the pump performance curve. Energy-recovery outputs should include high-pressure brine inlet and outlet conditions, recovered hydraulic power, transfer efficiency, pressure-transfer losses, leakage or mixing losses where relevant and residual energy discharged from the system. The results should quantify gross high-pressure pumping demand, recovered power, auxiliary pumping requirements and net plant electrical demand. Specific energy consumption should be reported for individual subsystems and for the complete desalination process on a product-water basis. The calculation should also identify whether pumps and recovery devices operate within recommended ranges, how performance changes at partial load, and the energy penalty associated with throttling, pressure losses, inefficient equipment or changing feed conditions.",
