  "icbcText": "For this suite, initial and boundary conditions are better described as economic initial conditions and horizon/constraint conditions rather than physical PDE boundary conditions. Initial-condition equations specify initial CAPEX, financing structure, debt/equity, initial electricity and chemical prices, initial production, discount rate, inflation rate, tax assumptions and asset value. Horizon and boundary equations comprise project-lifetime condition, terminal/salvage-value condition, minimum production constraint, maximum water-cost constraint, debt-service constraints, replacement schedules, capacity limits, resource-price scenarios, emissions constraints and terminal cash-flow conditions.",
  "calibration": "Calibrate equipment-cost correlations, installation factors, membrane-replacement rates, chemical consumption, labour, maintenance, electricity consumption, capacity factor, construction escalation and other empirical cost factors against recent completed projects, vendor quotations, actual operating accounts and location-specific price indices. Economic assumptions such as discount rate, electricity price, plant life and financing structure should normally be treated as explicit scenarios rather than silently “calibrated.”",
  "verification": "Independently audit CAPEX and OPEX aggregation, annualization, discounting, depreciation/tax treatment where included, replacement schedules, inflation basis, currency/year normalization, water-production denominator and cash-flow timing. Test simple zero-discount, zero-escalation and constant-production cases against hand calculations. Relevant names include Discounted Cash-Flow Model, Net Present Value, Internal Rate of Return, Capital-Recovery-Factor Method, Levelized Cost of Water, Payback-Period Method and Monte Carlo Method.",
  "validation": "Back-cast the model against independently documented desalination projects and compare predicted versus realized CAPEX, annual OPEX, electricity consumption, chemical expenditure, membrane replacement and water-production cost. Then perform out-of-sample project validation, probabilistic uncertainty analysis and sensitivity/scenario testing for electricity prices, discount rates, membrane life, capacity factor, inflation and plant utilization. Economic validation should report both prediction error and uncertainty ranges because an apparently precise single LCOW value can conceal much larger uncertainty.",
  "inputs": "Techno-economic analysis requires technical, capital, operating and financial information. Technical inputs include plant capacity, annual water production, plant availability, recovery, energy consumption, membrane quantity and area, membrane replacement frequency, pump and energy-recovery capacities, chemical consumption, labour requirements, maintenance requirements, intake and outfall characteristics and expected plant lifetime. Capital-cost inputs include land, intake and outfall construction, pretreatment equipment, membrane systems, pressure vessels, pumps, energy-recovery equipment, piping, electrical infrastructure, instrumentation, control systems, buildings, civil works, engineering, procurement, construction, commissioning and contingency allowances. Operating-cost inputs include electricity, thermal energy where applicable, membranes, filters, chemicals, labour, maintenance, laboratory analysis, waste disposal, sludge management, brine management, insurance and other recurring expenses. Financial inputs include construction period, project lifetime, discount rate, inflation, interest rate, financing structure, taxation, depreciation, residual value and financing charges. Revenue assumptions can include water selling price and income from recovered salts or minerals. Carbon pricing, emissions factors, subsidies and environmental charges may also be included. Finally, uncertainty analysis requires realistic ranges or probability distributions for important uncertain variables such as electricity prices, membrane lifetime, plant production, capital expenditure, chemical prices and financing conditions.",
  "outputs": "The required output should provide a transparent breakdown of the total cost of producing desalinated water throughout the project lifetime. Capital-cost outputs should distinguish intake and outfall infrastructure, civil works, pretreatment, membranes, pressure vessels, pumps, energy-recovery devices, thermal equipment where applicable, brine treatment, electrical systems, instrumentation, buildings, installation, engineering and contingencies. Operating-cost outputs should separately report electricity, thermal energy, membrane replacement, chemicals, cartridge filters, cleaning, labour, maintenance, waste disposal, brine management and other recurring costs. The analysis should report annual water production, annual expenditure, unit water-production cost, levelized cost of water, net present value, internal rate of return, payback period and other project-finance indicators where relevant. It should also provide membrane replacement schedules, equipment lifetime assumptions, financing assumptions, discount rate, inflation and electricity-price assumptions. Sensitivity results should show which parameters have the greatest effect on water cost, while scenario and uncertainty analyses should provide expected, favourable and adverse economic outcomes. Where environmental performance is included, the output should additionally report energy-related emissions, carbon intensity per unit of produced water, waste quantities, resource recovery revenues and the economic consequences of different brine-management strategies.",
